19.5.m:
Homage to your speech in favor;
homage to your speech turned away;
homage to your goodwill, O Death;
this homage to your ill-will!

People think of you as the earth;
I know you completely as Nirṛti from all sides;
you are in the past, possessing oblation; this is your portion;
with it let me worship you: svāhā!

You are the highest of plants;
the trees are your dependents;
let him be our dependent
who attacks us!

19.6.m:
I have brought you here; you have come within;
stand firm, unvacillating!
Let all the clans come to you;
let the realm not fall away from you!

Indra has held this one,
as firm with a firm oblation;
for him let Soma speak in favor,
and this Brahmaṇaspati!

Firm is heaven, firm is earth,
firm are the oceans, the mountains;
firm indeed by order, firm;
firm is this king of the clans!

The ocean rules over the flowing streams;
Agni is ruler of the earth;
the sun rules over the constellations;
you […]

Fasten on them a stag-skin,
put the fear of a deer [into them];
let the enemy flee turning away;
toward [us] let the cow come near!

19.7.m:
Agree together, unite together;
let your minds agree together,
as the former gods, in agreement,
attend their share!

Down in the cow-pen have the cows settled;
down have the wild beasts entered;
down have the banners of the folk [settled];
down have the unseen ones clung!

From the snow-covered [mountain] you flow forth;
you go to the river;
may the waters, the divine ones, verily to me
grant that!

Let the divine folk purify me;
let the wise purify [me] through thought;
let all beings purify me;
let the purifying wind purify me!

Let the purifying wind purify me
for resolve, for capacity, for living,
and long to see the sun!

19.8.m:
May both heaven and earth, the observant ones,
and the bright, great sacrificial fee bring me through;
let Soma and Agni take note according to their own nature;
may Vāyu, Savitṛ, and Bhaga come to me!

Again has mind, again has life come to us;
again has sight, again has purpose come;
may Vaiśvānara, our undeceived body-protector,
stand between [us] from misfortune and reproach!

With splendor, with milk, with bodies
have we united, with a benevolent mind;
may Tvaṣṭṛ, the generous giver, grant broad room;
let him mend what is broken of the body without lack!

We, O famed Agni, with fames,
as famed ones, would worship you with this oblation;
so establish for us the Indra-urged realm;
may we be in your sheltering advocacy!

We lead away your wrath;
let the two of us associate like two friends;
down beneath a heavy stone
do we press down your wrath!

Born on the mountain of the eagle's birth,
from the snow-covered [Himalaya],
toward it they go, famed with riches;
O kuṣṭha, come, destroyer of fever!

19.9.m:
Autumn, winter, cool season, spring,
summer, rainy season: place us in well-being;
assign us among cattle, among plants;
in a windless refuge indeed may we be in your shelter!

May Vaiśvānara with the Aṅgirases
arrange our chant of praise and sacrifice;
may he extend glory, heavenly light to them!

Puffing, they rise up
like cows from the stall;
look at the poison of the snakes;
there is no one to counteract it!

Homage to the black snake,
homage to the cross-striped one;
homage to the constrictor, to the brown one;
homage to the divine hosts!

19.10.m:
Besprinkle with the healing balm;
pour upon with the healing balm;
the healing balm is an auspicious medicine;
grant that to us for life!

May generous Indra make me renowned;
may Soma, Varuṇa, Vāyu, and Agni make [me] renowned!
May the god Savitṛ make me renowned;
may I be dear to the giver of the sacrificial fee!

Unto Indra, the renowned, with glories,
we, renowned, would worship with this oblation;
may he grant us dominion impelled by Indra;
may we be in his protecting advocacy!

Come hither, do not go away from us;
may Pūṣan make a pathlessness behind you!
Vāstoṣpati has called after you;
kinsmen, let your delight be in me!

I grasp your minds with my mind;
come unto my thought with your thoughts!
I place your heart under my control;
come following my path!

19.11.m:
You were bound fast to the iron post,
assailed by the deaths, which are a thousand;
let Yama give you back to me;
to that god we would worship with an oblation.

19.12.m:
The aśvattha tree mounted the śamī tree;
there the male-consecration was made;
that indeed is the remedy for it,
which they bring into women.

In the male indeed is the seed;
that is poured out into the woman;
that indeed is the acquisition of a son;
so Prajāpati declared.

Prajāpati approved;
Sinīvālī fashioned it;
placing the birth of females elsewhere,
may he place a male child here!

Let there be svadhā, O Mitra and Varuṇa, rich in offspring;
here swell the royal power with sweetness!
Drive away hatred and Nirṛti afar;
bestow upon him royal power, luster, and vigor!

The onset of this violent one is like that of fire;
and wailing like an intoxicated man he goes off;
to that brown, reddish fever
of fiery gifts be homage!

Homage to Yama, homage be to Death;
homage to King Varuṇa, the brilliant!
Homage to the Lord of the Field, homage to heaven;
homage to the earth, homage to the plants!

You, Indra, are an overlord eager for glory;
become the overcomer of men.
Rule over these divine clans;
may your dominion be long-lived and unaging!

In the eastern direction you, Indra, are overlord;
in the northern direction, O Vṛtra-slayer, you are the slayer of Dasyus.
Where the rivers flow, that is conquered by you;
in the south you are the bull to be invoked.

19.13.m:
You are the daughter of the Asuras,
you are the sister of the gods;
you were born from heaven and earth;
you have made the poison sapless.

Auspicious to us be the dove sent forth;
O gods, may the bird be harmless in our house!
For may Agni, the inspired sage, enjoy our oblation;
may the winged missile bypass us!

May the winged missile not harm us;
the hearth-seat makes its track in the fire-place.
Well-being to our cattle and to our men;
O gods, may the dove not injure us here!

19.14.m:
For me the waters set forth what is sweet;
for me Sūrya brought joy with his light;
for me the gods and the All-Gods born of heat [brought joy];
for me the divine Savitṛ provided wide expanse.

I uphold the earth and heaven;
I generated the seven streams together;
I speak whatever is truth and falsehood;
I [encompass] all speech and desire.

I encompass the earth and heaven;
I sent forth the seven seasons together;
I have encompassed all speech,
I who enjoyed Agnīṣomā as two comrades.

19.16.m:
Sky has stood firm, earth has stood firm,
this whole moving world has stood firm;
these mountains stand firm;
the horses have halted in their place.

O Jātavedas, turn [him] back;
let there be a hundred turnings for you,
a thousand returnings for you;
with them turn him back!

From here and from yonder, O Varuṇa,
ward off the evil;
extend wide protection;
ward the weapon far away!

Tvaṣṭṛ generated the wife;
Tvaṣṭṛ placed you for her as husband;
may Tvaṣṭṛ make for you two
a thousand lifespans, a long life!

Through the atmosphere he flies,
looking down upon all beings;
this is the greatness of the heavenly dog;
to him with this oblation I sacrifice.

In the waters is your birth, in heaven your seat,
in the ocean is your self, on earth your greatness;
this is the greatness of the heavenly dog;
to him with this oblation I sacrifice.

You are our lord of the mist;
maintain nourishment in our houses!
May prosperity come, may wealth come!

O god, increaser, you rule over thousandfold nourishment;
bestow that upon us; of that of yours may we partake;
sharing in that of yours may we be, svāhā!

19.17.m:
O wrist-amulet, open wide
the womb to make the embryo;
O boundary, place the son;
do you bring him, O arrival!

Come to us first, O wisdom,
with cattle, with horses;
you are in the rays of the sun;
you are worthy of sacrifice for us.

Place life in him, O Jātavedas;
O Tvaṣṭṛ, bestow offspring, power;
O Savitṛ, generate for him abundance of wealth;
may this [man] of yours live a hundred autumns!

Let Aditi shave the beard;
let the waters moisten it with luster;
let Prajāpati preserve him
to be well shaved again and again!

19.18.m:
Homage to your [arrow], O Rudra, as it is shot;
homage to those that are set [on the bowstring];
homage to those being released;
homage to those of yours that are drawn back!

Downward blows the wind;
downward burns the sun;
downward is the inviolable cow milked:
downward let your disease go!

The waters indeed are healing;
the waters are expellers of sickness;
let the waters going toward the ocean
carry away your disease!

The highest, the lowest,
and also the middle ones I will bind together;
Indra has encircled them with a bond;
bind them together, O Agni, you!

May Bṛhaspati make a fetter for you;
may Savitṛ make a fetter;
Indra and Agni [a fetter],
Bhaga and the Aśvins a fetter!

Set me for splendor among the brāhmaṇas;
set me for splendor among the kings;
for splendor among the commoners and the śūdras;
set in me splendor for splendor!

19.19.m:
May Soma protect us by day
from the noose of Death for well-being;
may the Waters protect us from the middle;
let Nirṛti not have power over us!

19.20.m:
Slay the moth, the borer, and the rat, O Aśvins;
break their head, crush their jaws and teeth,
so that the devourers do not eat our standing crop;
thus make safety for our grain!

Fly through the intermediate space;
do not fall upon the standing crop!
Sit upon the ridges of the mountains;
eat grass and skulls,
O locusts, as if [eating] of the settlers!

19.21.m:
Let truth alone conquer, not untruth;
let the path of truth be straight and good.
Speaking truth, let us attend to the firewood;
by truth heaven and earth spread out.

Make them turn back again;
bring them near again!
May Indra restrain them;
may Agni drive them near!

19.22.m:
May Vāyu bring them together;
may Tvaṣṭṛ maintain [them] for nourishment!
May Indra speak favorably for them;
may Rudra plan for abundance!

19.23.m:
Indeed, 'Overpowering' is your name;
you are born from overpower.
Indra is powerful among the gods;
we dig you up for overpowering.

Speak this away, O Maruts;
it is great Indra's repudiation.
This [plant] is worth a thousand;
this wards off poison.

19.24.m:
I place in front divine Intention, the fortunate;
let the mother of thought be easy of invocation for us.
Whatever region I go to, let it be entirely mine;
may I find her entered into my mind!

Let our mind return to us
for resolve, for capacity, for life,
and to see the sun for a long time!

Let the fathers, let the divine folk
give us back our mind;
may we attend upon the living band!

19.25.m:
Here are two lotuses, two snouts;
make it not a mosquito, O Galāpati!
Burn [it] from the fragile one!

19.26.m:
May I not commit theft against offspring;
may I not be deprived of what is learned!
Let not our toil be in vain;
O Agni, make it bring wealth!

With ghee one wins offspring;
with ghee one attains wealth;
with ghee, life-giving, splendor-giving,
one wins [favor] from the gods.

19.28.m:
Daring is the might of the daring one;
as it formerly stood,
great and delightful is Indra's [power].

To us [let] wisdom, good thought of all forms [come],
causing lofty songs to enter;
let her mark my many verses like cattle,
so that we may be attentive in the worlds!

19.29.m:
Let strength and vigor come to you from afar,
from heaven,
from the hills, from the mountains,
bestowing lifespan on you from every side!

19.31.m:
These vipers on the other bank / are cast-off sloughs;
with their slough we / wrap the two eyes
of the malevolent highwayman.

19.32.m:
May my mind be possessed of sight; / may my heart be possessed of sight;
may my thought be possessed of sight; / may my eye be possessed of sight;
place sight into my two eyes!

19.43.m:
Let the rivers flow together;
let the winds, let the winged [birds] meet together!
Promote this sacrifice with songs of praise;
I sacrifice with the confluent oblation!

19.44.m:
Dig out, break up, leave behind;
range right here, not carried off!
United am I with life; united with me is life!

Dig out, break up, leave behind;
range right here, not carried off!
United am I with splendor; united with me is splendor!

Dig out, break up, leave behind;
range right here, not carried off!
United am I with fiery energy; united with me is fiery energy!

Dig out, break up, leave behind;
range right here, not carried off!
United am I with offspring; united with me are offspring!

Dig out, break up, leave behind;
range right here, not carried off!
United am I with livestock; united with me is livestock!

Every evening Agni is our householder;
every morning he is the giver of benevolence.
Be the store of wealth for every good thing;
kindling you, may we nourish ourselves!

Every morning Agni is our householder;
every evening he is the giver of benevolence.
Be the store of wealth for every good thing;
kindling you, may we thrive for a hundred winters!

19.45.m:
Sweet blow the winds for the righteous man,
sweet flow the rivers;
sweet may the cows be for us!

Sweet be night and the dawns,
sweet the earthly realm;
sweet may the plants be for us!

Sweet be the lord of the forest for us,
sweet may the sun be;
sweet may Father Heaven be for us!

For you, waters, are bringing comfort;
so establish us in strength,
for great delight, for sight!

To that one we would go for you,
to whose dwelling you quicken [us];
and, waters, generate us [anew]!

This dappled cow has stepped forth;
she has sat again upon the mother
and upon the father, advancing to the light of heaven.

From his exhalation breathing out,
the luminous sphere moves within;
the buffalo looked abroad upon heaven.

He shines across the thirty domains;
speech has resorted to the winged bird
with days at the break of dawn.

The mighty one […] speech;
for broad speech was released;
the buffaloes made merry in heaven.

For the mighty one released speech;
he shines abroad according to the law of the domain;
unto the barhis […].

19.46.m:
You arose from the waters and from the sap of the earth;
you developed out of Viśvakarman.
Tvaṣṭṛ, disposing his form, goes;
knowing, [he knows] that divine birth of yours in the beginning.

Sixty, O adhvaryu, and ninety are the fetters
untied within the hotṛ, Agni;
they bind the simpleton, but the wise man passes beyond;
knowing, he follows the path of sacred order.

Here, when the distributor divides the oblation,
may he not deprive the rightful sharer nor strike him down;
nor may the one who was known to have a share become portionless, who has gone astray.

May the wind blow medicine,
healing and delighting, to our heart;
may it prolong our lives.

And you, O Wind, are our father,
and brother, and our friend;
so make us live.

You who are neither living nor dead,
you are the immortal embryo of the gods, O Sleep;
Varuṇānī is your mother, Yama your father.

The gods do not eat an oblation at his beginning;
he is excluded from the world of the well-doer.
He has chosen an evil thought with his own self;
for he is an evildoer among men.

Thus indeed the gods went out [from] this [man];
he reaches the [proper] world.
Not by your oblation [shall there be] dominion;
these mighty ones untie the fetter.

19.47.m:
The peacock eats the scorpion;
we eat the peacock.
Around him [is] the counter-remedy;
you are the crusher of scorpions.

The waters indeed are healing;
the waters are chasers of disease;
the waters are the medicine for everything;
the waters are crushers of scorpions.

You, O Agni, are the protector of vows,
a god among mortals;
you are to be praised at the sacrifices.

We have reached even the path of the gods,
to convey forth according to what we are able;
let Agni sacrifice, knowing, he indeed is the hotṛ;
he shall arrange the sacrificial rite, he the seasons.

Like cooked meat with a sword,
make [her] a bird-flight;
make [her] fly after the wild beasts;
make [her] vanish after the rays of light.

Seize the flesh;
strike down the body-hairs;
cast dust into the larynx, cut [it] off;
crush the one with upturned feet.

19.48.m:
Summon, O husband of the daughter;
summon, O brother's son-in-law;
behind you arose [he who is];
strike the sheep of the Kiṣkindhas.

In Nārada, in the gambler, in Varuṇa, O Soma,
they sought great greatness;
Viśvakarman found it in the ocean;
I place upon him the clatter of the dice in this [man].

I have forsaken you with the mind,
I have forsaken you with the heart;
I have forsaken you with all limbs;
for thus has the abandonment been made.

Heaven and Earth have made abandonment for me,
the divine Savitṛ, Bṛhaspati [has made] abandonment;
and Agni and Sūrya for you;
upon the bamboo point an abandonment has been made.

Place upon me the fortune of Dhātṛ;
make me an overlord through fortune;
may the bounteous Indra, ruler of the peoples,
lead me with glory.

In me is that lofty virility,
in me is skill and resolve;
may the gharma shine forth upon me thrice;
may I attain the milking [of abundance] through the gāyatrī metre, through the radiant light.

19.49.m:
We merchants call upon Indra;
may he, knowing, go before us as our savior;
smiting the foe, the highwayman, the wild beast,
may he, the ruler, be the giver of wealth to me.

May our barter be a forward barter and sale;
may return-barter make us wealthy;
sharing together, let them enjoy the oblation;
may our going out and coming up bring fortune.

That is indeed no garment
in which they pierce a man;
its form is smeared with blood;
therefore, it must be removed.

This is to be received with prayer;
thereby he makes it auspicious.
He is freed from the fetter of Calotropis;
thereby the giver looks around.
